data: {"seq":592,"ts":"2026-08-25T05:19:32.649Z","text":"The L2TP command injection path requires authentication (HttpAccessPermit) and the value flows through nvram/usrconf into uclited's l2tpCmdReq → l2tpFormatCmd → system. But it needs authenticated login. Let me trace where the L2TP user/password come from in uclited. Actually the cfg structure a1+284/a1+540 in l2tpFormatCmd are user/password. These get sent to system as part of the xl2tpd command line. This is a plausible command injection IF attacker can control username/password without filtration. But this is an authenticated path.\n\nGiven dynamic verification is hard (all network services fail to run in sandbox), and httpd won't listen, I should focus on what can be dynamically verified or is clearly a real vulnerability.\n\nLet me reconsider. The uclited has StartMiniServer as final handler - it's an HTTP server? Actually uclited binds a TCP port. Let me look at what the L2TP cfg data source is - is it from network or from nvram? The attack surface showed l2tpCmdReq as not a top path.\n\nLet me reconsider the whole approach. The high-scoring paths were all ffmpeg/libavformat (RTSP/UDP client) and xl2tpd. These are OTS components. Actually the ff_rtsp_connect strcpy overflow is real but requires device to connect to attacker RTSP server—weak reachability.\n\nLet me step back and think about what's genuinely the best target. TP-Link Archer C7. The httpd has userRpm pages. Many are authenticated. But the DoS/known vulns... \n\nActually, let me focus on dynamic verification. The network services don't listen in sandbox because they fail on startup (firmware check, no IP). But fw_request_trace connects to a mapped port. The empty diff means request didn't reach handler. This is because the binary didn't actually start (qemu detached). The trace tool maps port but the process doesn't run the handler.\n\nGiven constraints, I should focus on a finding I can verify statically with high confidence and give call chain + PoC. The L2TP command injection is a real path: HTTP POST /userRpm/L2TPCfgRpm.htm with L2TPName/L2TPPwd → strncpy into s (0xFF capped, no shell metachar filter) → swSetL2tpCfg → later uclited l2tpCmdReq/l2tpFormatCmd → system(\"xl2tpd -c /tmp/l2tpd.conf user \\\"%s\\\" password \\\"%s\\\" ...\"). The username string containing `\";` etc would inject.\n\nBut wait - is there sanitization? swChkL2tpDomain is called but not necessarily filtering shell chars. Let me verify how user/password flow: strncpy into s[71] and s[135], then swSetL2tpCfg(s) writes to nvram. Then when connecting, uclited rea","stream":true,"block":0}

event: thinking
data: {"seq":593,"ts":"2026-08-25T05:19:33.159Z","text":"The L2TP command injection path requires authentication (HttpAccessPermit) and the value flows through nvram/usrconf into uclited's l2tpCmdReq → l2tpFormatCmd → system. But it needs authenticated login. Let me trace where the L2TP user/password come from in uclited. Actually the cfg structure a1+284/a1+540 in l2tpFormatCmd are user/password. These get sent to system as part of the xl2tpd command line. This is a plausible command injection IF attacker can control username/password without filtration. But this is an authenticated path.\n\nGiven dynamic verification is hard (all network services fail to run in sandbox), and httpd won't listen, I should focus on what can be dynamically verified or is clearly a real vulnerability.\n\nLet me reconsider. The uclited has StartMiniServer as final handler - it's an HTTP server? Actually uclited binds a TCP port. Let me look at what the L2TP cfg data source is - is it from network or from nvram? The attack surface showed l2tpCmdReq as not a top path.\n\nLet me reconsider the whole approach. The high-scoring paths were all ffmpeg/libavformat (RTSP/UDP client) and xl2tpd. These are OTS components. Actually the ff_rtsp_connect strcpy overflow is real but requires device to connect to attacker RTSP server—weak reachability.\n\nLet me step back and think about what's genuinely the best target. TP-Link Archer C7. The httpd has userRpm pages. Many are authenticated. But the DoS/known vulns... \n\nActually, let me focus on dynamic verification. The network services don't listen in sandbox because they fail on startup (firmware check, no IP). But fw_request_trace connects to a mapped port. The empty diff means request didn't reach handler. This is because the binary didn't actually start (qemu detached). The trace tool maps port but the process doesn't run the handler.\n\nGiven constraints, I should focus on a finding I can verify statically with high confidence and give call chain + PoC. The L2TP command injection is a real path: HTTP POST /userRpm/L2TPCfgRpm.htm with L2TPName/L2TPPwd → strncpy into s (0xFF capped, no shell metachar filter) → swSetL2tpCfg → later uclited l2tpCmdReq/l2tpFormatCmd → system(\"xl2tpd -c /tmp/l2tpd.conf user \\\"%s\\\" password \\\"%s\\\" ...\"). The username string containing `\";` etc would inject.\n\nBut wait - is there sanitization? swChkL2tpDomain is called but not necessarily filtering shell chars. Let me verify how user/password flow: strncpy into s[71] and s[135], then swSetL2tpCfg(s) writes to nvram. Then when connecting, uclited reads cfg and formats command. No shell escaping seen. This matches the well-known TP-Link L2TP command injection (CVE). \n\nHowever for this to be exploitable, attacker needs admin","stream":true,"block":0}

event: thinking
data: {"seq":594,"ts":"2026-08-25T05:19:33.555Z","text":"The L2TP command injection path requires authentication (HttpAccessPermit) and the value flows through nvram/usrconf into uclited's l2tpCmdReq → l2tpFormatCmd → system. But it needs authenticated login. Let me trace where the L2TP user/password come from in uclited. Actually the cfg structure a1+284/a1+540 in l2tpFormatCmd are user/password. These get sent to system as part of the xl2tpd command line. This is a plausible command injection IF attacker can control username/password without filtration. But this is an authenticated path.\n\nGiven dynamic verification is hard (all network services fail to run in sandbox), and httpd won't listen, I should focus on what can be dynamically verified or is clearly a real vulnerability.\n\nLet me reconsider. The uclited has StartMiniServer as final handler - it's an HTTP server? Actually uclited binds a TCP port. Let me look at what the L2TP cfg data source is - is it from network or from nvram? The attack surface showed l2tpCmdReq as not a top path.\n\nLet me reconsider the whole approach. The high-scoring paths were all ffmpeg/libavformat (RTSP/UDP client) and xl2tpd. These are OTS components. Actually the ff_rtsp_connect strcpy overflow is real but requires device to connect to attacker RTSP server—weak reachability.\n\nLet me step back and think about what's genuinely the best target. TP-Link Archer C7. The httpd has userRpm pages. Many are authenticated. But the DoS/known vulns... \n\nActually, let me focus on dynamic verification. The network services don't listen in sandbox because they fail on startup (firmware check, no IP). But fw_request_trace connects to a mapped port. The empty diff means request didn't reach handler. This is because the binary didn't actually start (qemu detached). The trace tool maps port but the process doesn't run the handler.\n\nGiven constraints, I should focus on a finding I can verify statically with high confidence and give call chain + PoC. The L2TP command injection is a real path: HTTP POST /userRpm/L2TPCfgRpm.htm with L2TPName/L2TPPwd → strncpy into s (0xFF capped, no shell metachar filter) → swSetL2tpCfg → later uclited l2tpCmdReq/l2tpFormatCmd → system(\"xl2tpd -c /tmp/l2tpd.conf user \\\"%s\\\" password \\\"%s\\\" ...\"). The username string containing `\";` etc would inject.\n\nBut wait - is there sanitization? swChkL2tpDomain is called but not necessarily filtering shell chars. Let me verify how user/password flow: strncpy into s[71] and s[135], then swSetL2tpCfg(s) writes to nvram. Then when connecting, uclited reads cfg and formats command. No shell escaping seen. This matches the well-known TP-Link L2TP command injection (CVE). \n\nHowever for this to be exploitable, attacker needs admin credentials. That's a real but authenticated vuln.\n\nGiv","stream":true,"block":0}

