in + PoC. The L2TP command injection is a real path: HTTP POST /userRpm/L2TPCfgRpm.htm with L2TPName/L2TPPwd → strncpy into s (0xFF capped, no shell metachar filter) → swSetL2tpCfg → later uclited l2tpCmdReq/l2tpFormatCmd → system(\"xl2tpd -c /tmp/l2tpd.conf user \\\"%s\\\" password \\\"%s\\\" ...\"). The username string containing `\";` etc would inject.\n\nBut wait - is there sanitization? swChkL2tpDomain is called but not necessarily filtering shell chars. Let me verify how user/password flow: strncpy into s[71] and s[135], then swSetL2tpCfg(s) writes to nvram. Then when connecting, uclited reads cfg and formats command. No shell escaping seen. This matches the well-known TP-Link L2TP command injection (CVE). \n\nHowever for this to be exploitable, attacker needs admin credentials. That's a real but authenticated vuln.\n\nGiven the sandbox can't run these daemons (dynamic verification unavailable for them), and my instructions say observed/verified need a real trace_i","stream":true,"block":0}

