event: thinking
data: {"seq":785,"ts":"2026-08-25T05:23:49.512Z","text":"Let me check the dnsproxy structure size. The request structure is at least 6100+ bytes (a1+6100 used, a1+6096 counter). So name decode writes into a large structure. The `sub_4028D8(a1, &v6)` in dns_decode_request writes name to a1 (offset 0). The structure is large (>6100). A DNS name max is 255 bytes in standard, but the decoder has no limit - it accumulates labels until a 0 byte. But since recvfrom reads at most 0x200 (512) bytes into offset 5584 (a2+1396*4=5584), the name decode reads from that 512-byte buffer and writes decoded name to a1+0 previously.\n\nActually in dns_decode_request, `*(a1+6100) = a1 + 5584` points read cursor into the received buffer (a1+5584). sub_402E18 parses, sub_4028D8 decodes the name writing to a1 (offset 0) with v7 unbounded. The received buffer is 0x200 bytes at a1+5584. So a decoded name from a 512-byte buffer could write up to ~512 bytes into a1+0... but a1 is the same structure, offset 0. The structure is huge, so writing to a1+0","stream":true,"block":0}

event: thinking
data: {"seq":786,"ts":"2026-08-25T05:23:49.863Z","text":"Let me check the dnsproxy structure size. The request structure is at least 6100+ bytes (a1+6100 used, a1+6096 counter). So name decode writes into a large structure. The `sub_4028D8(a1, &v6)` in dns_decode_request writes name to a1 (offset 0). The structure is large (>6100). A DNS name max is 255 bytes in standard, but the decoder has no limit - it accumulates labels until a 0 byte. But since recvfrom reads at most 0x200 (512) bytes into offset 5584 (a2+1396*4=5584), the name decode reads from that 512-byte buffer and writes decoded name to a1+0 previously.\n\nActually in dns_decode_request, `*(a1+6100) = a1 + 5584` points read cursor into the received buffer (a1+5584). sub_402E18 parses, sub_4028D8 decodes the name writing to a1 (offset 0) with v7 unbounded. The received buffer is 0x200 bytes at a1+5584. So a decoded name from a 512-byte buffer could write up to ~512 bytes into a1+0... but a1 is the same structure, offset 0. The structure is huge, so writing to a1+0..a1+512 is within the struct. Not really ove","stream":true,"block":0}

event: thinking
data: {"seq":787,"ts":"2026-08-25T05:23:50.898Z","text":"Let me check the dnsproxy structure size. The request structure is at least 6100+ bytes (a1+6100 used, a1+6096 counter). So name decode writes into a large structure. The `sub_4028D8(a1, &v6)` in dns_decode_request writes name to a1 (offset 0). The structure is large (>6100). A DNS name max is 255 bytes in standard, but the decoder has no limit - it accumulates labels until a 0 byte. But since recvfrom reads at most 0x200 (512) bytes into offset 5584 (a2+1396*4=5584), the name decode reads from that 512-byte buffer and writes decoded name to a1+0 previously.\n\nActually in dns_decode_request, `*(a1+6100) = a1 + 5584` points read cursor into the received buffer (a1+5584). sub_402E18 parses, sub_4028D8 decodes the name writing to a1 (offset 0) with v7 unbounded. The received buffer is 0x200 bytes at a1+5584. So a decoded name from a 512-byte buffer could write up to ~512 bytes into a1+0... but a1 is the same structure, offset 0. The structure is huge, so writing to a1+0..a1+512 is within the struct. Not really overflow.\n\nLet me not overclaim. The dnsproxy structure is large. Writing decoded name to a1+0 with v7 up to ~512 do","stream":true,"block":0}

event: thinking
data: {"seq":788,"ts":"2026-08-25T05:23:51.462Z","text":"Let me check the dnsproxy structure size. The request structure is at least 6100+ bytes (a1+6100 used, a1+6096 counter). So name decode writes into a large structure. The `sub_4028D8(a1, &v6)` in dns_decode_request writes name to a1 (offset 0). The structure is large (>6100). A DNS name max is 255 bytes in standard, but the decoder has no limit - it accumulates labels until a 0 byte. But since recvfrom reads at most 0x200 (512) bytes into offset 5584 (a2+1396*4=5584), the name decode reads from that 512-byte buffer and writes decoded name to a1+0 previously.\n\nActually in dns_decode_request, `*(a1+6100) = a1 + 5584` points read cursor into the received buffer (a1+5584). sub_402E18 parses, sub_4028D8 decodes the name writing to a1 (offset 0) with v7 unbounded. The received buffer is 0x200 bytes at a1+5584. So a decoded name from a 512-byte buffer could write up to ~512 bytes into a1+0... but a1 is the same structure, offset 0. The structure is huge, so writing to a1+0..a1+512 is within the struct. Not really overflow.\n\nLet me not overclaim. The dnsproxy structure is large. Writing decoded name to a1+0 with v7 up to ~512 doesn't overflow the multiple-KB struct.\n\nThe more interesting is sub_402A54's memcpy at a1+266 with rdata length, bounded by 0x100 and 512 checks.\n\nActually let me reconsider. dnsproxy may not be the best. Given dynamic limitation, let me focus on things I can actually verify or have strong static proof.\n\nLet me reconsider the libavformat route. Actually, let me reconsider - the attack surface top paths were ff_rtsp_connect strcpy (stack overflow) and ff_udp_set_remote_url / udp_open memcpy. These are in libavformat which is used by minidlnad (DLNA). minidlnad parses media. But ff_rtsp_connect is client for RTSP streaming, reachable only if router connects out.\n\nGiven time budget and that dynamic verification is mostly unavailable","stream":true,"block":0}

event: thinking
data: {"seq":789,"ts":"2026-08-25T05:23:51.817Z","text":"Let me check the dnsproxy structure size. The request structure is at least 6100+ bytes (a1+6100 used, a1+6096 counter). So name decode writes into a large structure. The `sub_4028D8(a1, &v6)` in dns_decode_request writes name to a1 (offset 0). The structure is large (>6100). A DNS name max is 255 bytes in standard, but the decoder has no limit - it accumulates labels until a 0 byte. But since recvfrom reads at most 0x200 (512) bytes into offset 5584 (a2+1396*4=5584), the name decode reads from that 512-byte buffer and writes decoded name to a1+0 previously.\n\nActually in dns_decode_request, `*(a1+6100) = a1 + 5584` points read cursor into the received buffer (a1+5584). sub_402E18 parses, sub_4028D8 decodes the name writing to a1 (offset 0) with v7 unbounded. The received buffer is 0x200 bytes at a1+5584. So a decoded name from a 512-byte buffer could write up to ~512 bytes into a1+0... but a1 is the same structure, offset 0. The structure is huge, so writing to a1+0..a1+512 is within the struct. Not really overflow.\n\nLet me not overclaim. The dnsproxy structure is large. Writing decoded name to a1+0 with v7 up to ~512 doesn't overflow the multiple-KB struct.\n\nThe more interesting is sub_402A54's memcpy at a1+266 with rdata length, bounded by 0x100 and 512 checks.\n\nActually let me reconsider. dnsproxy may not be the best. Given dynamic limitation, let me focus on things I can actually verify or have strong static proof.\n\nLet me reconsider the libavformat route. Actually, let me reconsider - the attack surface top paths were ff_rtsp_connect strcpy (stack overflow) and ff_udp_set_remote_url / udp_open memcpy. These are in libavformat which is used by minidlnad (DLNA). minidlnad parses media. But ff_rtsp_connect is client for RTSP streaming, reachable only if router connects out.\n\nGiven time budget and that dynamic verification is mostly unavailable in this sandbox, I have one strong static finding recorded. Let me try one more dynamic angle that's likely to work: use qemu_exec to feed crafted DNS packet via stdin to dnsproxy isn't possible (r","stream":true,"block":0}

event: thinking
data: {"seq":790,"ts":"2026-08-25T05:23:52.446Z","text":"Let me check the dnsproxy structure size. The request structure is at least 6100+ bytes (a1+6100 used, a1+6096 counter). So name decode writes into a large structure. The `sub_4028D8(a1, &v6)` in dns_decode_request writes name to a1 (offset 0). The structure is large (>6100). A DNS name max is 255 bytes in standard, but the decoder has no limit - it accumulates labels until a 0 byte. But since recvfrom reads at most 0x200 (512) bytes into offset 5584 (a2+1396*4=5584), the name decode reads from that 512-byte buffer and writes decoded name to a1+0 previously.\n\nActually in dns_decode_request, `*(a1+6100) = a1 + 5584` points read cursor into the received buffer (a1+5584). sub_402E18 parses, sub_4028D8 decodes the name writing to a1 (offset 0) with v7 unbounded. The received buffer is 0x200 bytes at a1+5584. So a decoded name from a 512-byte buffer could write up to ~512 bytes into a1+0... but a1 is the same structure, offset 0. The structure is huge, so writing to a1+0..a1+512 is within the struct. Not really overflow.\n\nLet me not overclaim. The dnsproxy structure is large. Writing decoded name to a1+0 with v7 up to ~512 doesn't overflow the multiple-KB struct.\n\nThe more interesting is sub_402A54's memcpy at a1+266 with rdata length, bounded by 0x100 and 512 checks.\n\nActually let me reconsider. dnsproxy may not be the best. Given dynamic limitation, let me focus on things I can actually verify or have strong static proof.\n\nLet me reconsider the libavformat route. Actually, let me reconsider - the attack surface top paths were ff_rtsp_connect strcpy (stack overflow) and ff_udp_set_remote_url / udp_open memcpy. These are in libavformat which is used by minidlnad (DLNA). minidlnad parses media. But ff_rtsp_connect is client for RTSP streaming, reachable only if router connects out.\n\nGiven time budget and that dynamic verification is mostly unavailable in this sandbox, I have one strong static finding recorded. Let me try one more dynamic angle that's likely to work: use qemu_exec to feed crafted DNS packet via stdin to dnsproxy isn't possible (reads from socket). \n\nLet me try the ipcserver or another simple daemon that reads stdin. Actually, let me reconsider httpd - it failed firmware check. But minidlnad failed due to no IP. \n\nLet me try to see if I can get dnsproxy to actually process via qemu_exec with proper args to print usage, or try a direct file-b","stream":true,"block":0}

