data: {"seq":798,"ts":"2026-08-25T05:23:56.113Z","text":"Let me check the dnsproxy structure size. The request structure is at least 6100+ bytes (a1+6100 used, a1+6096 counter). So name decode writes into a large structure. The `sub_4028D8(a1, &v6)` in dns_decode_request writes name to a1 (offset 0). The structure is large (>6100). A DNS name max is 255 bytes in standard, but the decoder has no limit - it accumulates labels until a 0 byte. But since recvfrom reads at most 0x200 (512) bytes into offset 5584 (a2+1396*4=5584), the name decode reads from that 512-byte buffer and writes decoded name to a1+0 previously.\n\nActually in dns_decode_request, `*(a1+6100) = a1 + 5584` points read cursor into the received buffer (a1+5584). sub_402E18 parses, sub_4028D8 decodes the name writing to a1 (offset 0) with v7 unbounded. The received buffer is 0x200 bytes at a1+5584. So a decoded name from a 512-byte buffer could write up to ~512 bytes into a1+0... but a1 is the same structure, offset 0. The structure is huge, so writing to a1+0..a1+512 is within the struct. Not really overflow.\n\nLet me not overclaim. The dnsproxy structure is large. Writing decoded name to a1+0 with v7 up to ~512 doesn't overflow the multiple-KB struct.\n\nThe more interesting is sub_402A54's memcpy at a1+266 with rdata length, bounded by 0x100 and 512 checks.\n\nActually let me reconsider. dnsproxy may not be the best. Given dynamic limitation, let me focus on things I can actually verify or have strong static proof.\n\nLet me reconsider the libavformat route. Actually, let me reconsider - the attack surface top paths were ff_rtsp_connect strcpy (stack overflow) and ff_udp_set_remote_url / udp_open memcpy. These are in libavformat which is used by minidlnad (DLNA). minidlnad parses media. But ff_rtsp_connect is client for RTSP streaming, reachable only if router connects out.\n\nGiven time budget and that dynamic verification is mostly unavailable in this sandbox, I have one strong static finding recorded. Let me try one more dynamic angle that's likely to work: use qemu_exec to feed crafted DNS packet via stdin to dnsproxy isn't possible (reads from socket). \n\nLet me try the ipcserver or another simple daemon that reads stdin. Actually, let me reconsider httpd - it failed firmware check. But minidlnad failed due to no IP. \n\nLet me try to see if I can get dnsproxy to actually process via qemu_exec with proper args to print usage, or try a direct file-based parser binary. \n\nActually, let me reconsider. Given the budget of 24 qemu exec and 192 traces, and I've used several. Let me do a focused qemu_exec on dnsproxy feeding a malformed DNS packet through stdin — but it's socket based.\n\nLet me instead check whether there's a better dynamic target. Let me record the dnsproxy name-decode issue only if I can bound it. Otherwise skip.\n\nLet me try a different approach - run fuzz not available. Let me try frida on httpd? No, httpd can't start.\n\nGiven constraints, I think I should also look at the bpalogin or ipcserver which may read stdin. Let me check ipcserver since it's a server that might process network input via stdin in qemu.\n\nActually, given the substantial difficulty of dynamic verification in this sandbox for these daemons, and I've already recorded one solid finding, let me do one more focused dynamic attempt and then conclude.\n\nLet me try the minidlnad with a config/bind argument to see if it can start listening. Actually it needs IP detection. \n\nLet me try dnsproxy reading from a file via stdin override - not applicable.\n\nI'll try to verify the dnsproxy overflow hypothesis by using qemu_exec with argv that makes it read from stdin (some dnsproxy builds support reading). Unlikely.\n\nLet me instead run one more fuzz attempt targeting a specific function (dns_decode_request) with args, since whole-binary failed due to afl missing.\n\nActually afl-fuzz is missing entirely. So fuzz is unavailable.\n\nLet me reconsider: the strongest fully-verifiable thing I can do is record the dnsproxy memory issue as static-only with appropriate confidence, IF I establish the structure bounds. The structure is ~6200 bytes. Writing decoded name (max ~512 bytes from the 0x200 receive buffer) into offset 0 is safe. So dnsproxy name decode is probably NOT exploitable as overflow.\n\nLet me instead verify whether httpd can be traced with a proper argv approach. httpd failed \"firmw","stream":true,"block":0}

