orded. Let me try one more dynamic angle that's likely to work: use qemu_exec to feed crafted DNS packet via stdin to dnsproxy isn't possible (reads from socket). \n\nLet me try the ipcserver or another simple daemon that reads stdin. Actually, let me reconsider httpd - it failed firmware check. But minidlnad failed due to no IP. \n\nLet me try to see if I can get dnsproxy to actually process via qemu_exec with proper args to print usage, or try a direct file-based parser binary. \n\nActually, let me reconsider. Given the budget of 24 qemu exec and 192 traces, and I've used several. Let me do a focused qemu_exec on dnsproxy feeding a malformed DNS packet through stdin — but it's socket based.\n\nLet me instead check whether there's a better dynamic target. Let me record the dnsproxy name-decode issue only if I can bound it. Otherwise skip.\n\nLet me try a different approach - run fuzz not available. Let me try frida on httpd? No, httpd can't start.\n\nGiven constraints, I think I should also look at the bpalogin or ipcserver which may read stdin. Let me check ipcserver since it's a server that might process network input via stdin in qemu.\n\nActually, given the substantial difficulty of dynamic verification in this sandbox for these daemons, and I've already recorded one solid finding, let me do one more focused dynamic attempt and then conclude.\n\nLet me try the minidlnad with a config/bind argument to see if it can start listening. Actually it needs IP detection. \n\nLet me try dnsproxy reading from a file via stdin override - not applicable.\n\nI'll try to verify the dnsproxy overflow hypothesis by using qemu_exec with argv that makes it read from stdin (some dnsproxy builds support reading). Unlikely.\n\nLet me instead run one more fuzz attempt targeting a specific function (dns_decode_request) with args, since whole-binary failed due to afl missing.\n\nActually afl-fuzz is missing entirely. So fuzz is unavailable.\n\nLet me reconsider: the strongest fully-verifiable thing I can do is record the dnsproxy memory issue as static-only with appropriate confidence, IF I establish the structure bounds. The structure is ~6200 bytes. Writing decoded name (max ~512 bytes from the 0x200 receive buffer) into offset 0 is safe. So dnsproxy name decode is probably NOT exploitable as overflow.\n\nLet me instead verify whether httpd can be traced with a proper argv approach. httpd failed \"firmware version check\" so no.\n\nI think the best remaining move: examine the libavformat ff_rtsp_connect for a truly reachable overflow, or examine another network daemon that might actually run in qemu.\n\nLet me try athadhoc (IN-001, UDP) — a small daemon, might start. Or check bpalogin. \n\nActually, let me step back and try to get ANY dynamic signal. Let me run dhcp6c or dhcp6s with qemu to see if they parse from stdin. dhcp6s is a DHCPv6 server processing packets.\n\nGiven budget, let me try one trace on a service that might actually bind. Let me test if uclited can bind - it's a big daemon. Actually let me just conclude with static findings plus the one recorded, and consider recording the ff_rtsp_connect overflow since it's on the high-score attack surface.\n\nLet me look more carefully at ff_rtsp_connect's strcpy(dest, &s[1601]). dest is 64 bytes, source is RTSP server response data stored in buffer s (the response buffer), offset 1601. s is the buffer for the OPTIONS response. s[1601] is the \"Server:\" line content or similar. This is data from a malicious RTSP server. But the router would need to connect out to an attacker RTSP server. minidlnad doesn't do RTSP client typically. This is a library function; reachability questionable for network input. Score 6.05 but attribution static, ai_review dataflow unclear. Not strong enough to record confidently.\n\nLet me make a decision: I've done substantial work. The sandbox cannot run most network daemons (firmware version check, no IP, missing modules). Dynamic verification is fundamentally constrained. I recorded one solid authenticated command injection.\n\nLet me do a couple more useful static verifications and record the dnsproxy candidate only if I can bound the overflow. Let me get the size of the receive/request struct in dnsproxy to settle this. Actually the key: is there any path where decoded name writes beyond a fixed struct? Given the struct is huge, no.\n\nLet me pivot to examine the dropbear SSH (IN-013) — it's a real service that could parse via stdin perhaps. But dropbear is standard hardened.\n\nActually, given the environment constraints, let me verify if I can get a trace on ipcserver reading stdin, or just conclude.\n\nLet me try one thing: feed a DNS packet to dnsproxy via qemu_exec with stdin to see if a startup with a mode reads stdin. Probably not.\n\nI'll do a final useful action: try to dynamically verify via qemu_exec the minidlnad by giving it a config that lets it bind, OR check that I can crash the dnsproxy decoder via a crafted packet loaded from /tmp file if dnsproxy has a file-reading test mode.\n\nI think the responsible conclusion is: dynamic verification of the network daemons in this sandbox is not feasible (all daemons fail to bind/start due to hardware deps). I recorded the command injection. Let me record the dnsproxy name-decoder issue with a bounded, honest assessment OR skip it.\n\nLet me actually check the size of dnsproxy receive structure more concretely to decide. dns_read_packet: a2[1524] = recvfrom(a1, a2+1396, 0x200, ...). a2+1396 in _DWORD units = byte offset 5584. And a2+1524 = byte 6096. So structure >= 6096+4 bytes. The receive buffer is at 5584..6096 (512 bytes). Name decode writes to a1 (offset 0). So decoded name at offset 0 could overwrite struct fields but stays within the 6KB struct. Not an exploitable overflow unless the decoder writes > ~5584 bytes, impossible from 512-byte input.\n\nSo dnsproxy name decoder is NOT a vulnerability (writes into large struct). Good to not record.\n\nSo my command injection finding stands. Let me look for one more solid memory corruption that's actually within-bounds-danger. \n\nThe pppSetWanParameter in httpd uses getWanStaticIpIfName() with sprintf into 132-byte buffer - if ifname controllable... but it's internal.\n\nLet me reconsider the xl2tpd network_thread strcpy(j+916, v10) overflow (score 6.05, ai CWE-121). network_thread is in atend. xl2tpd (md5 08b08601) is excluded as not-public but it IS a network daemon on UDP 1701. Let me look at network_thread and do_control strcpy.\n\nActually xl2tpd IS in excluded list (not-public). So out of scope for public attack.\n\nI think I've reached a reasonable conclusion. Let me do one more scan of high-scored memunsafe paths in a relevant reachable binary. But given the sandbox limits for dynamic, and I have one good finding, let me try to strengthen with one more dynamic attempt then conclude.\n\nLet me try to actually get httpd to run by providing the config flash - probably not possible.\n\nFinal: Let me try to dynamically validate the L2TP finding chain is at least partially reachable by testing if uclited can start and accept connections (it's the daemon that runs system()). If uclited starts, I could potentially trigger. But it needs config too.\n\nI'll conclude after summarizing. Let me just make sure I haven't missed an obviously exploitable unauthenticated memory corruption in a daemon that can run. Let me quickly check ipcserver since it might read from stdin meaningfully.","stream":false,"block":0}

event: text
data: {"seq":827,"ts":"2026-08-25T05:24:10.602Z","text":"dnsproxy/其他网络 daemon 在本沙箱普遍因固件依赖无法真正监听，动态验证受限。让我确认 dnsproxy 接收结构大小，以判定 name 解码器 `sub_4028D8` 的越界是否真实可达——结构体至少 6KB，name 解码写入 offset 0，理论上不越界。这轮确认后我做总结。\n\n同时快速检查 ipcserver（简单 daemon，可能能跑）作为替代动态入口。\n\n","stream":false,"block":1}

